\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

Output-distribution alignment is the dominant design dimension for PPL (signal type: up to 97.5\% of the gap at 30\%; upper bound, not fully orthogonal); cross-layer coupling contributes 10--47\% on downstream tasks (3-seed $K{=}4$-corrected; commonsense 22--47\%, GSM8K 10\%); MMLU anomalously favors $K{=}4$, reflecting allocation alignment confirmed by constraint analysis (Appendices~\ref{app:k4_downstream},~\ref{app:constraint}).
A predictor ($<$1\% of base) reduces TEAL's PPL by 14.5\% via masks 46--68\% divergent from magnitude; KL outperforms TEAL on commonsense (ARC-c, WG, BoolQ) but underperforms on knowledge tasks (MMLU, GSM8K) across tested scales---a learned allocation trade-off, not an inherent objective limitation (Appendix~\ref{app:constraint}).

\paragraph{Target-Swap Ablation: Target Quality vs.\ Gradient Coupling.}
To disentangle whether KL's advantage stems from its coupled gradient pathway or from the mask patterns it discovers, we train a standard BCE predictor on KL-derived oracle masks instead of magnitude-based masks (C1 target-swap ablation).
Table~\ref{tab:c1} reports results under TEAL uniform allocation at matched sparsity.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Target-swap ablation (C1): BCE on KL-derived masks vs.\ KL predictor (same seed), TEAL uniform. All seeds show max $\Delta$$\leq$1.70pp at 30\%; max $\Delta$$\leq$0.79pp at 50\% (s42). $^\dagger$s42 oracle is from an earlier training run; s123/s456 use matched retrained oracles.}
\label{tab:c1}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{
\begin{tabular}{lllccccc}
\toprule
Sparsity & Seed & Method & PPL$\downarrow$ & ARC-c & WG & MMLU & GSM8K \\
\midrule
\multirow{6}{*}{30\%}
& s42$^\dagger$ & C1 & 9.45 & 40.27 & 67.40 & \textbf{42.30} & \textbf{7.58} \\ % exp_id: c1_ablation_30pct_s42_eval_v2
& & KL & \textbf{8.80} & \textbf{40.78} & \textbf{68.59} & 40.6 & 7.35 \\ % exp_id: bench_kl30_uniform
& s123 & C1 & 8.84 & 44.71 & \textbf{69.77} & \textbf{39.92} & 6.82 \\ % exp_id: eval_c1_retrain_30pct_s123
& & KL & \textbf{8.83} & \textbf{45.31} & 69.38 & 39.4 & \textbf{7.51} \\ % exp_id: kl30_s123_eval
& s456 & C1 & 8.82 & 44.71 & \textbf{69.93} & 41.04 & 6.60 \\ % exp_id: eval_c1_retrain_30pct_s456
& & KL & \textbf{8.79} & \textbf{45.05} & 68.75 & \textbf{42.0} & \textbf{7.58} \\ % exp_id: kl30_s456_uniform_eval
\midrule
\multirow{2}{*}{50\%}
& s42 & C1 & 11.96 & 32.17 & \textbf{63.14} & 27.79 & \textbf{2.05} \\ % exp_id: c1_bce_kl_targets_teal50u
& & KL & 11.96 & \textbf{32.59} & 62.35 & \textbf{27.92} & 1.97 \\ % exp_id: c1_kl_teal50u
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

At 50\%, C1 matches KL within 0.79pp, while magnitude-trained BCE yields $2.4\times$ worse PPL (Table~\ref{tab:main}).
At 30\%, retrained seeds show $\leq$1.18pp agreement; the s42 oracle from an earlier checkpoint has a 0.65pp gap but supports the core finding.
C1 demonstrates that KL-discovered mask patterns \emph{transfer} their quality advantage to a simpler training mechanism (PPL within 0.79pp; downstream variation up to 4.5pp MMLU), consistent with masks as the proximate quality driver. However, KL's coupled optimization remains necessary for \emph{discovering} these patterns: a per-layer KL control (\S\ref{sec:perlayer}) shows that cross-layer coupling contributes marginally to PPL but substantially to downstream fidelity. % exp_id: bce50_3seed, c1_bce_kl_targets_30u, c1_s123

\paragraph{Soft-vs-Hard Confound.}
C1 also changes mask representation (hard vs.\ soft), but BCE+Gumbel (32.22) vs.\ standard BCE (27.97) suggests softness alone does not explain KL's advantage; trajectory-dependent confounds are discussed in Appendix~\ref{app:oracle_provenance}. % exp_id: bce_gumbel_no_penalty_s42, eval_bce_v3_s42

